7592186045417] [17592186231523 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186231792 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231523 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050226 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231523 #edb/ref 1050 1500.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231523 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "ae3b271d-f536-5674-bc90-24bf860c235c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231792 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186231793 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231792 #edb/ref 1061 602721 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231792 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "09eed649-fd35-5633-ab45-26f9ff4df9a4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231792 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186231991 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231793 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186231796 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231793 #edb/ref 1061 602722 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231793 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7d00c846-017e-5944-a878-8692d8e20406" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231793 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186231407 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231794 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186231795 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231794 #edb/ref 1061 602725 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231794 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6b69ecfa-55d3-5851-a962-974fd7edd06b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231794 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186231408 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231795 #edb/ref 1061 602729 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231795 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "dfab021e-6bf7-5ab4-8bd9-2af9f811ab5b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231795 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186231409 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231796 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186231820 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231796 #edb/ref 1061 603155 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231796 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "10bd959b-3db6-58ef-8e40-eabd05f87aab" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231796 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186231994 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231820 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186231794 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231820 #edb/ref 1061 608077 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231820 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a6ed1a27-61c7-514f-ab23-a91a7edba38d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231820 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186232020 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231991 #edb/ref 1064 "In this lesson, we will use Kirchhoff's voltage law to derive a second-order differential equation that governs the current $I(t)$ in an RCL circuit.\n\n![](images/d1/d1ce9424aca366d9028319fa2035e01339b26963e39150a8b14b8f2b34d67138.png)\n\nIn a series RCL circuit, Kirchhoff's voltage law states that the source voltage equals the sum of the voltage drops:\n\n$$\nE(t)=V_R+V_C+V_L\n$$\n\nFor the three components:\n\n- Resistor: the voltage drop is proportional to the current,\n\n$$\nV_R=RI.\n$$\n\n- Capacitor: the charge $q$ and capacitor voltage satisfy $q=CV_C,$ so\n\n$$\nV_C=\\dfrac{q}{C}.\n$$\n\n- Inductor: the voltage drop is proportional to the rate of change of current,\n\n$$\nV_L=L\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into Kirchhoff's voltage law gives\n\n$$\nE=RI+\\dfrac{q}{C}+L\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}.\n$$\n\nTo obtain a differential equation in terms of $I$ only, we first differentiate both sides with respect to $t{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dt}(E) & =\\frac{d}{dt}(RI+\\frac{q}{C}+L\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}) \\\\ \\frac{dE}{dt} & =R\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+\\frac{1}{C}\\cdot \\frac{dq}{dt}+L\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that the current $I(t),$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q(t),$ measured in coulombs:\n\n$$\nI=\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nSince $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}=I,$ we get the second-order differential equation governing the current:\n\n$$\nL\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^2I}{\\mathrm{d}t^2}+R\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}+\\dfrac{1}{C}I=\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}E}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nFinally, dividing both sides by $L$ gives an equivalent form:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^2I}{\\mathrm{d}t^2}+\\dfrac{R}{L}\\cdot\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}+\\dfrac{1}{LC}I=\\dfrac{1}{L} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}E}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nLet's see a more concrete derivation in the next example." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231991 #edb/ref 1065 14791 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231991 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "e9875681-c31f-535a-b3e2-7fcec32a16e3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231991 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231994 #edb/ref 1064 "When we solve a second-order differential equation for the current $I(t),$ we often write the solution in the form\n\n$$\nI(t)=I_c(t)+I_p(t),\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $I_c(t)$ is the **transient current**, which decays to $0$ as $t\\to\\infty,$ and\n\n- $I_p(t)$ is the **steady-state current**, which governs the current’s long-term behavior.\n\nTo determine a unique solution for $I(t),$ we must specify two initial conditions, typically $I(0)$ and $I'(0).$\n\nThe current is often given directly as an initial condition, for example, $I(0)=I_0.$ We are also often given the initial *capacitor charge*, $q(0)=q_0.$\n\nTo find the other condition $I'(0),$ we use Kirchhoff's voltage law equation:\n\n$$\nE(t) = RI+\\dfrac{q}{C}+L\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nSolving for $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t} = \\dfrac{1}{L}E - \\dfrac{R}{L}I - \\dfrac{q}{LC}.\n$$\n\nNow evaluate at $t=0{:}$\n\n$$\nI'(0) = \\dfrac{1}{L}E(0) - \\dfrac{R}{L}I(0) - \\dfrac{1}{LC}q(0)\n$$\n\nSo, if the initial charge $q(0)$ and initial current $I(0)$ are given, we can compute $I'(0)$ by substituting those values into the formula above.\n\nIn the next slide, we'll determine the initial conditions of a differential equation that governs the current in a given circuit." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231994 #edb/ref 1065 14808 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231994 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "a6f06e46-ed1e-5e49-a9f8-679033a08a0f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231994 #edb/ref 1067 "Initial Conditions for Solving the ODE Governing the Current in an RCL Circuit" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186232020 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose a circuit with a constant EMF of $E=8\\,\\textrm{V},$ resistance of $R=6\\,\\Omega,$ capacitance of $C=0.25\\,\\textrm{F},$ and inductance of $L=2\\,\\textrm{H},$ satisfies Kirchhoff's voltage law equation\n\n$$\n8=6I+4q+2\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's derive the differential equation for $I$ by differentiating both sides with respect to $t{:}$\n\n$$\n0=6\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}+4\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}+2\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^2I}{\\mathrm{d}t^2}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}=I,$ the differential equation for $I$ is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^2I}{\\mathrm{d}t^2}+3\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}+2I=0.\n$$\n\nNow suppose the initial charge and current are $q(0)=\\dfrac12$ and $I(0)=1,$ respectively. Solving our first relation for $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t} = 4-3I-2q.\n$$\n\nThen, the initial conditions for the current we use to solve the ODE are $I(0)=1$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}^{′}(0) & =4-3I(0)-2q(0) \\\\  & =4-3\\cdot 1-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the resulting initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^2I}{\\mathrm{d}t^2}+3\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}+2I=0, \\qquad I(0) = 1, \\quad I'(0) = 0,\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\nI(t)=2e^{-t}-e^{-2t}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186232020 #edb/ref 1065 14901 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186232020 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "02b0c1dd-4819-5337-bc05-870ded85d834" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186232020 #edb/ref 1067 "A Worked Example" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372918 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186372919 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372918 #edb/ref 1069 "q-345229" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372918 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372918 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/7a/7abc70a954ec2bb29c909f92b5a88655f8f3c66270ca0947487a8bc5f5e4deda.png)\n\nFind an equation for the current (in amperes) in the circuit given in the diagram above, expressing your answer in terms of time $t$ (in seconds) only, and given that the initial charge on the capacitor is $\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{C}$ and the initial current is $2\\,\\text{A}.$\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372918 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0fe4da9b-3724-51c1-9459-24000ff26a62" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372918 #edb/ref 1074 "The diagram shows a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $24\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $12\\,Ω,$ a capacitor of capacitance $\\frac{1}{8}\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductor of inductance $4\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=12\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=12I.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{\\frac{1}{8}}=8q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=4\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=4\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, these values are related by the equation\n\n$$\n24=12I+8q+4\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating with respect to $t,$ the differential equation governing the current in the circuit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dt}(24) & =\\frac{d}{dt}(12I+8q+4\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}) \\\\ 0 & =12\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+8\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+4\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, when substituting in $I=\\frac{dq}{dt},$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+3\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+2I=0.\n$$\n\nThe given equation is a second-order linear homogeneous equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must take a linear combination of two linearly independent solutions.\n\nAssuming the solutions take the form $I={e}^{\\lambda t},$ we obtain two solutions, namely, $I={e}^{-t}$ and $I={e}^{-2t}.$\nSo, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)=A{e}^{-t}+B{e}^{-2t}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the circuit starts with an initial charge of $\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{C}$ on the capacitor and an initial current of $2\\,\\text{A}.$\nNow, solving the Kirchhoff's law equation for $\\frac{dI}{dt},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}=6-3I-2q.\n$$\n\nSo, we can apply the initial conditions $I(0)=2$ and \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}^{′}(0) & =6-3I(0)-2q(0) \\\\  & =6-3\\cdot 2-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solve for $A$ and $B.$\n\n- Substituting $I(0)=2$ into our general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =A{e}^{-0}+B{e}^{-0} \\\\ 2 & =A+B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Differentiating $I,$ we get\n\n$$\n{I}^{′}=-A{e}^{-t}-2B{e}^{-2t}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in ${I}^{′}(0)=-1$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & =-A{e}^{-0}-2B{e}^{-0} \\\\ -1 & =-A-2B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPutting the two equations in $A$ and $B$ together gives the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2 &=A+B \\\\ -1 &=-A-2B\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $A=3$ and $B=-1.$ Therefore, the equation for the current (in amperes) in the circuit is\n\n$$\nI(t)=3{e}^{-t}-{e}^{-2t}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186372919 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372920 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372919 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372921 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372919 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372922 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372919 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372923 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372919 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372924 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372919 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372919 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372919 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186372922 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372919 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "1b2a0949-4487-5bb2-8e80-6a5b82332303" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372920 #edb/ref 1080 "I(t)=2{e}^{-t}-{e}^{-2t}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372920 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372920 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "b81dfb34-9217-5a69-8561-4b65c7914497" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372921 #edb/ref 1080 "I(t)=3{e}^{-t}+{e}^{-2t}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372921 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372921 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "77d0f34f-558a-5689-958c-34b9638e23cf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372922 #edb/ref 1080 "I(t)=3{e}^{-t}-{e}^{-2t}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372922 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372922 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "add80056-050c-5e32-a37c-5b8a0a3a9050" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372923 #edb/ref 1080 "I(t)=3{e}^{-2t}-{e}^{-t}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372923 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372923 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "6566a78d-f159-569f-b85a-15985ad98001" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372924 #edb/ref 1080 "I(t)=-{e}^{-t}+3{e}^{-2t}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372924 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372924 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "fdec70c8-fc9a-599a-919d-eae57f93b88c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372925 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186372926 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372925 #edb/ref 1069 "q-345230" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372925 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372925 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/0b/0b6e5f0ccf9a4f34ad60e82306db85fce99816de088bf66c90fcaf1a5bac10ed.png)\n\nFind an equation for the current (in amperes) in the circuit given in the diagram above, expressing your answer in terms of time $t$ (in seconds) only, and given that the initial charge on the capacitor is $\\frac{2}{3}\\,\\text{C}$ and the initial current is $1\\,\\text{A}.$\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372925 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ec7eedce-fc35-5301-816a-4bb7cc4477d9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372925 #edb/ref 1074 "The diagram shows a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $3\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $5\\,Ω,$ a capacitor of capacitance $\\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductor of inductance $2\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=5\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=5I.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{\\frac{1}{3}}=3q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=2\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=2\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, these values are related by the equation\n\n$$\n3=5I+3q+2\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating with respect to $t,$ the differential equation governing the current in the circuit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dt}(3) & =\\frac{d}{dt}(5I+3q+2\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}) \\\\ 0 & =5\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+3\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+2\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, when substituting in $I=\\frac{dq}{dt},$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot \\frac{dI}{dt}+\\frac{3}{2}I=0.\n$$\n\nThe given equation is a second-order linear homogeneous equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must take a linear combination of two linearly independent solutions.\n\nAssuming the solutions take the form $I={e}^{\\lambda t},$ we obtain two solutions, namely, $I={e}^{-3t/2}$ and $I={e}^{-t}.$\nSo, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)=A{e}^{-3t/2}+B{e}^{-t}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the circuit starts with an initial charge of $\\frac{2}{3}\\,\\text{C}$ on the capacitor and an initial current of $1\\,\\text{A}.$\nNow, solving the Kirchhoff's law equation for $\\frac{dI}{dt},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}=\\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{5}{2}I-\\frac{3}{2}q.\n$$\n\nSo, we can apply the initial conditions $I(0)=1$ and \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}^{′}(0) & =\\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{5}{2}I(0)-\\frac{3}{2}q(0) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot 1-\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & =-2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solve for $A$ and $B.$\n\n- Substituting $I(0)=1$ into our general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =A{e}^{-3(0)/2}+B{e}^{-0} \\\\ 1 & =A+B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Differentiating $I,$ we get\n\n$$\n{I}^{′}=-\\frac{3}{2}A{e}^{-3t/2}-B{e}^{-t}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in ${I}^{′}(0)=-2$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2 & =-\\frac{3}{2}A{e}^{-3(0)/2}-B{e}^{-0} \\\\ -2 & =-\\frac{3}{2}A-B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPutting the two equations in $A$ and $B$ together gives the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}1 &=A+B \\\\ -2 &=-\\frac{3}{2}A-B\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $A=2$ and $B=-1.$ Therefore, the equation for the current (in amperes) in the circuit is\n\n$$\nI(t)=2{e}^{-3t/2}-{e}^{-t}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186372926 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372927 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372926 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372928 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372926 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372929 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372926 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372930 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372926 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372931 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372926 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372926 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372926 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186372929 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372926 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "c7592773-6af2-5116-b635-86d74e652343" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372927 #edb/ref 1080 "I(t)=2{e}^{-t}-{e}^{-3t/2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372927 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372927 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "e35d4f95-5cc9-5b1e-948f-3f8ad0b14282" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372928 #edb/ref 1080 "I(t)=2{e}^{-3t/2}+{e}^{-t}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372928 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372928 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "d707e33c-e0a0-557c-9b4b-002604e3e4d9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372929 #edb/ref 1080 "I(t)=2{e}^{-3t/2}-{e}^{-t}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372929 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372929 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "10b84a1a-1126-5e1b-a431-4ef4c7b12f9c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372930 #edb/ref 1080 "I(t)=-{e}^{-3t/2}+2{e}^{-t}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372930 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372930 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "c7754268-15f7-568c-a6e2-7efbca7c488b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372931 #edb/ref 1080 "I(t)={e}^{-3t/2}-2{e}^{-t}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372931 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372931 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "a4acd14b-68f1-542b-bec8-cf4f2be4c069" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385927 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186385928 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385927 #edb/ref 1069 "q-345394" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385927 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385927 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/53/5322f43ff1eeee6e06b3c393cc1b59c9af32bc2e7dba66766607af117954ace1.png)\n\nFind, in amperes, the amplitude of the steady state current for the RCL circuit shown above.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385927 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "87500776-9edf-567b-97b4-f626c972de46" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385927 #edb/ref 1074 "The diagram shows a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $20\\,Ω,$ a capacitor of capacitance $0.1\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductor of inductance $10\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=20\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=20I.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{0.1}=10q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=10\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=10\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, these values are related by the equation\n\n$$\n8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)=20I+10q+10\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating with respect to $t,$ the differential equation governing the current in the circuit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dt}(8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)) & =\\frac{d}{dt}(20I+10q+10\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}) \\\\ -8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) & =20\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+10\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+10\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, when substituting in $I=\\frac{dq}{dt},$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+2\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+I=-\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t).\n$$\n\nThe given equation is a second-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${I}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}{I}_{c}}{d{t}^{2}}+2\\,\\frac{d{I}_{c}}{dt}+{I}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as a trial solution, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-t}+Bt{e}^{-t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants.\n\n- Next, we find a particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute this, as well as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d{I}_{p}}{dt} & =-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t), \\\\ \\frac{{d}^{2}{I}_{p}}{d{t}^{2}} & =-\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t))+2(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)) \\\\ +(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)) & =-\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) \\\\ (2\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+(-2\\alpha )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) & =-\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2\\beta &=0\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)) \\\\ -2\\alpha &=-\\frac{4}{5}\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t))\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =\\frac{2}{5}$ and $\\beta =0.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=\\frac{2}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)={I}_{c}(t)+{I}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-t}+Bt{e}^{-t}+\\frac{2}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-t}+Bt{e}^{-t}$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The function ${I}_{p}(t)=\\frac{2}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)$ is the *steady-state current*. It governs the current's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nI(t)≈{I}_{p}(t)=\\frac{2}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state current, in amperes, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{(\\frac{2}{5})}^{2}+{0}^{2}}=\\frac{2}{5}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186385928 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186385929 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385928 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186385930 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385928 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186385931 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385928 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186385932 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385928 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186385933 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385928 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385928 #edb/